1,'The design of this case is so odd and poorly thought out. Did any real pump wearers test it? I can\'t imagine they did, because they would have caught this glaring flaw: While clipped horizontally to a waistband or belt, the clip is situated in the case so that the wearer must read/interact with the screen upside-down. WHAT?!?!?! Why would the designer think it was important for the people looking AT you to be able to read the screen right-side-up? The wearer is the important one here, not her/his audience. (And, no, there is no way to flip the clip around to the other side - it is either vertical or horizontal one-way only.) Since the integration with the Dexcom G5 just rolled out, I look at my pump frequently, as it now displays my CGM graph. Having an upside-down screen is even MORE annoying now that I rely on those quick glances to see how I\'m doing.<br /><br />Also, the clip needs teeth or something to actually hold it in place. My pump slips off of my waistband several times per day (and while sleeping), no matter what type of material my pants are made of. I have had too many slip-offs, with the only thing holding my pump to my body being the tubing to my infusion set (ummm... OUCH). I still keep the case on for its protection, but usually end up slipping the whole thing into my pocket because it\'s just so annoyingly-designed.<br /><br />The only thing I like about it is the color. But, that\'s it.<br /><br />I can\'t wait until I have more options for a case.',B01NB16DW9,AGBMK4R56WS5SDUDHH73VNXVZ7VQ
