5,'The product arrived quickly and was as advertised.  I thought I\'d try sodium alginate after reading that it\'s the main ingredient of Gaviscon  Advance.  Several studies have indicated that Gaviscon Advance (G.A.) is the most effective treatment for LPR.  (Disclaimer: I\'m not a doctor) Since it\'s only sold in Europe, G.A.  is difficult and costly to acquire in the States.  My DIY version is simple:  about a 2:1 ratio of sodium alginate to baking soda in a small amount of water.  That\'s about 1/8 tsp sodium alginate in 1 tablespoon of water.  I have to add the sodium alginate very slowly to the liquid, as it globs easily.  I use a shot glass, and stir rapidly with a chop-stick while shaking the powder in.  Then I let sit for at least half an hour to fully gel up.  Ideally, it should be about the consistency of molasses.  I take it just before bed, and  other times as needed.  Along with avoiding acid foods and eating smaller portions, sodium alginate has effectively controlled my symptoms of LPR and related cough.  It\'s a good product, and 4 oz. has lasted a long time. I\'ll be reordering soon.',B00LLO6FSK,AHAFWFVFLHD2FYGPG6DHWLTMROOQ
3,'My husband bought this as a gift for my because I have fibromyalgia and he was hoping this would help ease the aches in my swollen joints. It only allows you to go on the low pressure setting in one area, not the entire back. and it only moves up and down. it pushes way too hard, and made me hurt worse. My husband likes to use it while he does homework, but I can\'t use it at all with my fibro, it hurts my body so badly. We would have returned this product if my husband hadn\'t found it somewhat helpful for his neck and back tension.',B009WQRFMY,AF76JGOMNXEILOJNX5HNRZLAIJSA
1,'Maybe I got a bad batch, but these things rip apart very easy. Not impressed.',B08VGGQLYP,AHBFW4AEUZVNY2OJXSXD4M2CWX7A
4,'Review of Pearlicare Replacement Brush Heads (8 Pack) - Compatible With Phillips Sonicare Gum Health Electric Toothbrush<br /><br />The brushes are obviously in a different pattern and possibly of different materials than OEM but I have no idea if this matters so I obviously cannot determine if the performance is the same, better or worse than OEM.  My teeth feel the same with these as when using OEM.<br /><br />In response to an answered question I am using one right now with a newer model that is not listed in terms of compatibility (the ExpertClean 7500 or HX9690).  One difference seems to be that OEM brush heads have a microchip that is read by the toothbrush handle to determine suggested mode and to inform the user that the brush head should be replaced.  To me, this really doesn’t matter and not included the microchip undoubtedly results in a lower cost.<br /><br />I did find that these brush heads fit a little too tight onto the shaft from the toothbrush handle.  For cleaning and drying I like to remove the brush head each time and these take a bit more effort to remove.  Since there is not any ribbing or roughing on the brush head I invariably end up using the brushes to help brace the hand when removing the brush head.  I hope that the metal in the brush head is softer than that in the shaft of the handle to preclude wearing out the big ticket item.<br /><br />I would note that accounting for time zone differences (and dateline) the seller responded very quickly to my question.',B07ZWNCBBQ,AE2I4NY2F3ARD5T32WLZ7NMW4R7A
