5,'Omega-3 fatty acids are essential to human health and can only be obtained through one\'s diet. Fatty fish like cod, tuna and salmon are the best sources, but you have to be careful because these foods can be contaminated with mercury and other pollutants. You can also get Omega-3 in flaxseed, walnuts and soybeans, but who is really going to eat enough of these on a daily basis to get an adequate amount? I know I\'m not since I hate the bitter taste of walnuts and really don\'t like dried soybeans. Higher blood levels of Omega-3 essential fatty acids including EPA and DHA are associated with improved cognitive function and mood, protective properties for the heart, and possibly a positive impact on joints and flexibility for people with arthritis.<br /><br />Taking a supplement may be beneficial, but a lot of the cheaper ones may contain traces of dioxin, mercury, PCBs and other contaminants from the environment. Not to mention the fact that you burp up the taste for hours. After trying several inexpensive brands including a free full-size bottle I received at a health fair, I decided that not all Omega-3 fish oil supplements are created equal. This one is as pure as you can get, has really helped with my cognitive function and mood, and most importantly - eased the chronic pain from arthritis. I am really happy that I switched to this high quality product from TruFormula.',B00SMIGBJQ,AHB7VVRKHY64GZLNL3FN2BVUXVAQ
4,'Flavor is good and packaging is very nice...directions are clear and precise.  I recommend easing into this product if you are new to workout supplements. Start off with maybe half a dose to see how it affects you.',B01EIRCZYE,AG6MHXD7XQG7YF65SFVVXD4Q7G6Q
5,'So helpful',B00UB1HHY0,AHELUHAYLARF6E2LPNPFY6OS7LMQ
5,'Review of: Super Organics Red Reishi Mushroom Powder | Healthy Immunity - Organic, Vegan & Non-GMO, 4 Oz<br /><br />I’m really pleased with this product … I’ve used it with three different meals and the ‘Super Organics – Red Reishi Mushroom Powder’ has enhanced the flavor of each dish.<br /><br />My first use of the powder was in Turkey Sloppy Joes.  As you know, using turkey in any dish and you lose some flavor as opposed to using beef.  Adding the mushroom powder amped up the flavor and gave the mixture a deeper, almost beefier taste.<br /><br />The next use of the powder was as a topping on baked Zucchini.  But a tiny amount of olive oil on top of sliced Zucchini … sprinkle on a little mushroom powder and a little Romano cheese powder on the top and then baked … so good.  Not only tasty but also healthy for you.  Just remember, when using as a vegetable topping to use it sparingly, you don’t want to overwhelm the taste of the Zucchini or overwhelm the taste of the cheese.<br /><br />The third use of the powder was in Lentils.  Add a scope of mushroom powder to the Lentils (after they were cooked) and you can impart a ‘mushroomy’/almost beefy type flavor to the Lentils.  Cook the Lentils with some carrots, celery, onions and then add the mushroom powder … scope over some cooked rice and you have a full, delicious meal that even the most strident meat-eater will like.<br /><br />I haven’t tried it in tea or a smoothie yet … but I am looking at making a stew fairly soon and I know that I’ll be adding this powder to the stew.  I’ll need to experiment with the amount of powder to use as a stew needs a heavier/meatier taste than the other meals I’ve made.  Oh … and know that I think of it, maybe adding some of this powder to some Zucchini bread would be good.  Hey, there are all kinds of options for this powder.<br /><br />I can’t really comment to heavily on the ‘healthy immunity’ claims on the product page but as this product is organic, vegan, non-gmo and gluten free and doesn’t list any negative ingredients or additives then the product is probably as healthy as fresh mushrooms.<br /><br />If you want a little extra flavor with your meals then this powder is something you should consider.',B07VRRK186,AGQIAEHZCIQ4F5O6TKP6YJA6V63Q
